For the record, here are the regular expressions which are applied to
the Newsgroups: header.  I'll let someone else expand this into an
actual list of groups:

	/alt\.sex/  
	/alt\.binaries\.pictures/ 
	/alt\.binaries\.sounds/ 
	/bisexu-l/  			(this matches a bitnet group)
	/motss/ 
	/rec\.arts\.erotica/  
	/soc\.bi/ 
	/nude/

The feed is currently sent through the state's educational network, so
it doesn't reach outside the state.  I don't know how many decoders
have been built but it's less than 50.  The project targets grade
schools and high schools (ages 6 and up, for those of you outside the
US), though some decoders have been placed in colleges.  All of these
sites are aware that they're getting partial feeds and are free to
obtain additional groups through other mechanisms.  I can only think of
one site that has gone to the trouble of setting up an outgoing feed.

News has *always* had the ability to restrict feeds, and almost every
feed we provide (around 30 altogether) has some restrictions on it.  No
RFC or other authority has ever promised people that only certain
feeding rules can be applied to crossposted articles.  All we've done
is to install an additional filter to fill a gap in the existing
software's capabilities.

Let me note that Olaf Titz, to whose article I'm responding, as well as
at least one other person posting in this thread, do not live in the
US.  Let me assure you:  Given the prevailing legal and political
climate, and also considering that Kentucky is smack in the "bible
belt", one simply *cannot* provide unrestricted news feeds to minors.
The _least_ that would happen is that we'd lose use of the TV signal,
which would be as good as shutting down the project completely.  And
how could this be better than filtering out a set of adult groups?
